## Package comparison

The Phase 3 package remains aligned with the approved plan, and this round stays within the allowed server/test files. The command-admission hook adds useful observation, but the focused test does not exercise competing work in the global queue and the phase still has mandatory gaps.

## Findings

### High — The new FIFO test serializes completion rather than testing the global queue

The test awaits `one.play()` to complete before invoking `two.pause()`. Consequently, the first job has left the coordinator before the second is admitted; matching hook/provider arrays proves ordinary sequential execution, not that commands from two sockets enter the one bounded global FIFO in observed order while work is pending. Block the first transport, submit both sockets, deterministically observe both admissions, release the provider, and compare admission and execution order.

### High — Actual acceptance-vs-shutdown interleaving remains unproved

Enrollment tests reject via synthetic `canEnroll` while the server is open, then close the server afterward. They do not exercise the production `closing` decision with a Node callback delivered around scope closure. The package requires deterministic evidence that this actual shutdown race either enrolls-and-finalizes or immediately destroys the socket.

### High — Blocked-command shutdown still lacks no-late-response evidence

The blocked-command test observes transport start and closes the client/graph, but does not await connection and forwarding finalizers, release provider work afterward, or assert the server write-attempt count remains unchanged. Client-side disposal and provider interruption do not prove no late response after provider release.

### High — Complete graph and connection finalization counts remain missing

The suite still does not instrument one coordinator or exact finalization of the complete two-client graph, pre-hello connection, and partial-frame connection. Socket destruction/path absence and aggregate provider counts cannot replace the package's exact listener, connection, forwarder, source, and Layer ownership evidence.

### High — Executable cleanup failure remains unverified at the process boundary

Signal-handler completion/interruption and direct Layer composition are covered, but no runtime executable-path test proves signal-driven dependency-order cleanup or that close/unlink failure sets nonzero process status while retaining tagged operation/message diagnostics.

### Medium — Blocked sampling and late-forwarder evidence remain partial

The blocked-sampling test has no connected socket or exact listener/connection cleanup counters. The late-event test emits only after the provider source is disposed and does not await forwarding finalizers, so it cannot isolate connection-child finalization from outer provider teardown.

### Medium — Most integration tests still lack failure-safe cleanup

The new FIFO test uses `try/finally`, but most earlier socket tests still release resources only on success paths. An intermediate assertion can leak sockets, listeners, or paths contrary to the package's focused-test cleanup requirement.

